---
{state.status === "retired" ? <StateEvent view={state} /> : <BaseLayout title={`${event.title} | The Catch`} current="Events">
  <div slot="locator" class="locator"><a href="/events/">Series</a><a href="/events/mail-voting/">Mail voting</a><span>September 14, 2026</span></div>
  <div class="content">
    <main class="story" id="story-root">
      <p class="story-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Supreme Court &middot; September 14, 2026</p>
      <h1 data-layer="fact">{event.title}</h1>
      <p class="story-dek" data-layer="fact">{event.dek}</p>
      <ReadingModes />

      <nav class="story-toc" aria-label="In this story" data-layer="fact">
        <details class="toc-disclosure" open><summary><span>In this story</span><span class="toc-current" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#what-happened">What happened</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-catch">The catch</a></li>
          <li><a href="#where-this-sits">Where this sits</a></li>
          <li><a href="#who-feels-it">Who feels it</a></li>
          <li><a href="#outlets">What the coverage got right, and what it got wrong</a></li>
          <li><a href="#claim-check">Claim checked against the record</a></li>
          <li><a href="#what-happened-next">What happened next</a></li>
          <li><a href="#unknowns">What we do not know yet</a></li>
          <li><a href="#records">The records</a></li>
        </ol>
      </details></nav>
      <div class="kpi-strip" data-layer="fact">
        {event.kpis.map((k) => (
          <div class="kpi">
            <span class="kpi-value">{k.value}<small>{k.unit ? ` ${k.unit}` : ""}</small></span>
            <span class="kpi-label">{k.label}</span>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <aside class="three-things" data-layer="fact">
        <p class="tt-label">Three things to know</p>
        <ol>
          <li>On Monday, September 14, the Supreme Court turned down the government's emergency request to lift a judge's block on the Postal Service's new ballot-mail rule. The unsigned order says the government "is unlikely to succeed" in its challenge to that block, and that the factors the Court weighs before granting emergency relief do not favor a stay, a stay being a pause on a lower court's order.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-docket-2026-09-14" passage="The Government is unlikely to succeed on the merits of its challenge to the District Court's preliminary injunction" /><Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="the Postal Service Federal Ballot Mail Portal mandatory for Ballot Mail" /></li>
          <li>The block comes from Judge Indira Talwani in Boston, dated September 4. It stops the Postal Service from enforcing the parts of the rule that make the approved envelopes, the voter barcodes and the online portal mandatory, for elections on or before November 3.<Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="elections occurring before or on November 3" /><Cite s="usps-final-rule" passage="will not be accepted" /></li>
          <li>Justice Brett Kavanaugh voted with the majority and wrote that there is "at least a fair prospect" the rule is within the Postal Service's legal power; his objection was that officials lack the time to carry it out this year.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-order" passage="fair prospect that" /> Justices Samuel Alito and Clarence Thomas would have lifted Talwani's block.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-order" passage="JUSTICE ALITO, with whom JUSTICE THOMAS joins" /> The government's own filing says Nichols's order still bars the Postal Service from implementing and enforcing the rule.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-supplemental" passage="D.D.C. injunction bars the Postal Service from implementing and enforcing" /> A pause in Boston, it told the Court, would still have required a parallel pause in Washington.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-supplemental" passage="a parallel stay" /> The Court did not decide whether the rule is lawful, and the thing blocked is, in Alito's words, "a final rule concerning election ballot mail", the Postal Service's rule rather than the March executive order behind it.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-order" passage="a final rule concerning election ballot mail" /></li>
        </ol>
      </aside>
      <p class="railed rail-note" data-layer="fact">Story updated {event.updated}. Since the Court's refusal, the Postal Service has posted that it is not enforcing the rule for 2026, and the Postmaster General has said work on the portal has stopped.<Cite s="usps-election-mail-page-2026-09-18" passage="Postal Service is not enforcing the federal ballot mail rule for 2026" /><Cite s="ap-2026-09-17" passage="has stopped work on developing" /></p>

      <div data-layer="proof" class="event-record-context">
        <p class="sources-line">This story rests on the Court's September 14 order and the docket as updated that day, Judge Talwani's September 4 injunction and her August 25 order finding a violation, the First Circuit's September 10 order, Judge Carl Nichols's September 13 opinion in Washington, the executive order, the Postal Service's rule and its September 1 and September 4 statements, the government's September 6 application and the briefs answering it, the Postal Service's election-mail page as it read on September 18, the Associated Press interview of September 17, the inspector general's September 4 primary-mail audit, and coverage checked against those records. The days on this page are counted from those dates.</p>
      </div>

      <section id="what-happened">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The request, the order, and the two signed opinions</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>

        <p data-layer="narrative">On September 14 the Supreme Court refused the government's emergency request to pause Talwani's block while the appeal goes on. The request had been presented to Justice Ketanji Brown Jackson on September 6, and she referred it to the full Court. The order is unsigned and three sentences long, and the docket entry for that night is the order itself.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-docket-2026-09-14" passage="Application (26A305) for stay presented to Justice Jackson and by her referred to the Court is denied" /><Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="September 4, 2026" /></p>
        <QuoteCard speaker="Supreme Court of the United States" when="September 14, 2026" where="Unsigned order on the government's request">
          <p>Application (26A305) for stay presented to Justice Jackson and by her referred to the Court is denied. The Government is unlikely to succeed on the merits of its challenge to the District Court's preliminary injunction. And the equitable factors applicable for obtaining emergency relief from this Court do not favor a stay.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-docket-2026-09-14" passage="Application (26A305) for stay presented to Justice Jackson and by her referred to the Court is denied" /></p>
        </QuoteCard>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Kavanaugh joined the refusal and wrote one paragraph saying why. He said the Postal Service may well have the legal power to issue the rule. He voted to leave the block in place because forcing the rule onto this year's elections would be "arbitrary and capricious", the Administrative Procedure Act's phrase for an agency decision that was not a reasoned one, since election officials lack the time to carry it out. His reason for the vote is the paragraph's second sentence.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-order" passage="fair prospect that" /><Cite s="scotus-26a305-order" passage="do not have sufficient time to" /></p>
        <QuoteCard speaker="Brett Kavanaugh, Supreme Court justice" when="September 14, 2026" where="Concurring in the refusal">
          <p>But applying the rule in the 2026 elections would be arbitrary and capricious in violation of the Administrative Procedure Act because state and local election officials do not have sufficient time to reasonably implement the rule before the elections.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-order" passage="do not have sufficient time to" /></p>
        </QuoteCard>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Alito, joined by Thomas, would have granted the pause. He calls the block "universal", meaning it reaches beyond the people who sued. He writes that some of the groups that sued likely lack standing, the legal right to bring the case, and that the states' remaining claim, that the agency acted with no legal power at all, is the kind this Court has recently called a Hail Mary pass. His opening sentence is the whole of his position.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-order" passage="Hail Mary pass" /></p>
        <QuoteCard speaker="Samuel Alito, Supreme Court justice" when="September 14, 2026" where="Dissenting, joined by Clarence Thomas">
          <p>The Court incorrectly denies the Government's application for a stay of a universal injunction that bars implementation of a final rule concerning election ballot mail.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-order" passage="The Court incorrectly denies" /></p>
        </QuoteCard>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The rule, issued August 21 and published August 26, requires official Election Mail logos, envelopes built for high-speed sorting, a unique barcode for every voter, and an online Federal Ballot Mail Portal where election offices upload each voter's name and address as printed on the outbound envelope, the issuing state, and the barcodes on both envelopes.<Cite s="usps-final-rule" passage="the name and address of the voter as found on Outbound Federal Ballot Mail envelope" /> On August 25 Talwani found that the defendants "violated the court's Preliminary Injunction" by completing the rule after her Aug. 11 order, and she imposed no further remedy.<Cite s="talwani-violation-2026-08-25" passage="violated the court's Preliminary Injunction" /> The Postal Service's notice for those records lists a purpose of helping determine adherence to federal law and facilitating law-enforcement efforts.<Cite s="usps-sorn-820-225" passage="federal law and facilitate law" /> The same notice says the records are kept for {computed.sornRetentionYears} years.<Cite s="usps-sorn-820-225" passage="Records are retained for 5 years." /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Outbound ballot mailings that do not comply "will not be accepted".<Cite s="usps-final-rule" passage="will not be accepted" /> Noncompliant mailings are returned to the authorized ballot mailer, an election official or a mail service provider that official has authorized to send the ballots.<Cite s="usps-final-rule" passage="returned to the authorized" /><Cite s="usps-final-rule" passage="such as a mail service provider, that an" /> The check did not apply to voters' returned ballots.<Cite s="usps-final-rule" passage="does not apply to Return" /> It covers federal general, special and runoff elections; ballots for military and overseas voters, and primaries, sit outside it.<Cite s="usps-final-rule" passage="federal general, special, or runoff elections" /><Cite s="usps-final-rule" passage="the rule will not apply to primaries" /> President Donald J. Trump's March 31 executive order directed the Postal Service to start the rulemaking; the Postal Service says the order did not mandate a final rule and that the finished rule was its own decision.<Cite s="usps-final-rule" passage="did not mandate a final" /> On August 24 the Court had paused an earlier block of the executive order itself, writing that courts review final rules and that if the Postal Service's rule harmed the states they could challenge that rule.<Cite s="scotus-26a124-opinion" passage="Federal courts review final rules, not" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Talwani's block covers the rule's mandatory requirements for elections on or before November 3 and does not decide the case. She wrote that her order does not stop states from voluntarily complying with the rule.<Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="elections occurring before or on November 3" /><Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="States from voluntarily complying with the Final Rule" /> Late on September 13, in a separate case in Washington, Judge Carl Nichols entered an order that blocks the Postal Service from "implementing and enforcing" the whole rule, with no election-date limit; his reason is that no statute gives the Postal Service the power to issue key parts of it, so the rule was likely issued without legal authority.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-supplemental" passage="from implementing and enforcing the Final Rule" /><Cite s="nichols-pi-2026-09-13" passage="the Postal Service the power to issue key parts of the Rule" /> The government told the Court the next morning that a win in Boston would still leave that second order to be lifted in Washington.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-supplemental" passage="a stay in this case will require the district" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The First Circuit had already refused to pause Talwani's block. On September 10, Judges Gustavo Gelpí, Julie Rikelman and Seth Aframe wrote that a general postal statute is a "wafer-thin reed" on which to hang election rules, and that the district court's findings about the harm of letting the rule take effect now, which they saw no error in, included the lack of a working portal.<Cite s="ca1-stay-denial-2026-09-10" passage="wafer-thin reed" /><Cite s="ca1-stay-denial-2026-09-10" passage="the lack of a presently operative portal" /> Four days later the Supreme Court reached the same result on the pause, and the block on this fall's mandate held at every level it was tested.<Cite s="ca1-stay-denial-2026-09-10" passage="The motions for a stay are denied" /><Cite s="scotus-26a305-docket-2026-09-14" passage="Application (26A305) for stay presented to Justice Jackson and by her referred to the Court is denied" /></p>

        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>From the executive order to the Court's refusal</strong><span>Dated steps in the orders, dockets and Postal Service statements.</span></figcaption>
          <DecisionTimeline entries={timeline} />
          <p class="chart-source">{computed.daysPiToDenial} days separate Talwani's block from the Court's refusal; the executive order that started the fight is {computed.daysEoToDenial} days older than the refusal.<Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="September 4, 2026" /><Cite s="scotus-26a305-order" passage="September 14, 2026" /><Cite s="eo-14399" passage="March 31, 2026" /></p>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section id="the-catch">
        <aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
          <p class="tt-label">The catch</p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <h3>WIRED credited Kavanaugh with the Court's own sentence.</h3>
              WIRED's sentence reads, in full: “The Government is unlikely to succeed on the merits of its challenge to the District Court’s preliminary injunction,” Justice Brett Kavanaugh wrote in a concurring opinion with Justice Ketanji Brown Jackson. In the order, that sentence sits above Kavanaugh's name, in the unsigned part the whole Court issued, and Jackson appears only as the justice the request was presented to. Kavanaugh's own paragraph points the other way: there is "at least a fair prospect" the rule is within the Postal Service's power. A reader of that dispatch comes away thinking two justices signed an opinion saying the government will lose, when the Court's words are narrower and Kavanaugh's are narrower still.<Cite s="wired-2026-09-14" passage="concurring opinion with Justice Ketanji Brown Jackson" /><Cite s="scotus-26a305-order" passage="fair prospect that" />
              <a class="catch-jump" href="#outlets">Coverage</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>WIRED and CNBC counted a 7-2 vote the order does not print.</h3>
              WIRED wrote that the court "was divided 7-2." CNBC, covering Trump's reaction, wrote that two public dissents "implies a 7-2 vote." The order names Kavanaugh concurring and Alito joined by Thomas dissenting; it names Jackson only as the justice the request went to, and it names no other justice's vote. CNBC says its count is an inference from the two public dissents; WIRED states the count without saying where it came from. The order neither states the count nor rules it out, so both cards below carry the same verdict: not in the record.<Cite s="wired-2026-09-14" passage="divided 7-2" /><Cite s="cnbc-trump-2026-09-15" passage="implies a 7-2 vote" /><Cite s="scotus-26a305-docket-2026-09-14" passage="Justice Alito, joined by Justice Thomas, dissenting" />
              <a class="catch-jump" href="#outlets">Coverage</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>BBC said the judge blocked Trump's order; the block is on the Postal Service's rule.</h3>
              BBC wrote that Talwani imposed an injunction "blocking Trump's order" on September 4, and that Alito and Thomas wrote the challenge to Trump's order was a "Hail Mary pass." Talwani's order blocks parts of the Postal Service's rule. Alito's Hail Mary line is about the states' claim against that rule. The Court had paused the block of the executive order itself on August 24. A reader of the BBC piece can think the justices just killed the March order, when what stays blocked is the rule, for elections through November 3.<Cite s="bbc-2026-09-15" passage="blocking Trump's order" /><Cite s="bbc-2026-09-15" passage="the challenge to Trump's order was a &quot;Hail Mary pass&quot;" /><Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="elections occurring before or on November 3" /><Cite s="scotus-26a124-opinion" passage="We agree and grant the stay." />
              <a class="catch-jump" href="#claim-check">Claim check</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Left out: eight of thirteen articles did not mention the second block.</h3>
              Nichols's September 13 order blocks the same rule from a second courtroom, with no election-date limit. We read each of the thirteen articles on this page for that order. Politico and BBC name Nichols; CBS wrote that a judge in Washington "also blocked the mail-in ballot rules on Sunday"; Roll Call wrote that "a second district court judge in Washington" ordered the rule paused; the Washington Examiner wrote that "a Trump-appointed judge granted voting rights groups and Democratic state officials a preliminary injunction on the new Postal Service rules". WIRED, ABC, both CNBC pieces, SCOTUSblog, both Associated Press pieces and NPR do not mention it. A reader of those eight can think Monday's refusal is the only thing keeping the rule off this fall's ballots, when a second order does the same work and reaches past November 3.<Cite s="politico-2026-09-14" passage="U.S. District Judge Carl Nichols said" /><Cite s="bbc-2026-09-15" passage="Judge Carl Nichols" /><Cite s="cbs-2026-09-14" passage="also blocked the mail-in ballot rules on Sunday" /><Cite s="rollcall-2026-09-14" passage="a second district court judge in Washington" /><Cite s="examiner-2026-09-14" passage="a Trump-appointed judge granted voting rights groups and Democratic state officials a preliminary injunction on the new Postal Service rules" /><Cite s="scotus-26a305-supplemental" passage="from implementing and enforcing the Final Rule" />
              <a class="catch-jump" href="#where-this-sits">Where this sits</a>
            </li>
          </ol>
          <p class="catch-minor">Smaller slips: ABC quoted Kavanaugh as writing "arbitrary and capricious violation," dropping the word in that the concurrence prints; the meaning is unchanged.<Cite s="abc-2026-09-14" passage="arbitrary and capricious violation of the Administrative Procedure Act" /><Cite s="scotus-26a305-order" passage="arbitrary and capricious" /></p>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="where-this-sits">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Two legal questions, and who has answered which</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Where this sits</h2>

        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>Days between the steps</strong><span>From Talwani's block to the election the injunction names.</span></figcaption>
          <DateLine {...dateLine} ariaLabel="Days between the dated steps" />
          <p class="chart-source">The Court's refusal came {computed.daysDenialToElection} days before November 3; the First Circuit's came {computed.daysCa1ToDenial} days before the Court's.<Cite s="usc-2-7" passage="Tuesday next after the 1st Monday in November" /><Cite s="scotus-26a305-order" passage="September 14, 2026" /><Cite s="ca1-stay-denial-2026-09-10" passage="Entered: September 10, 2026" /></p>
          <p class="chart-vintage" data-layer="proof">Day counts are calendar days between the dates at UTC midnight. November 3 minus September 14 is {computed.daysDenialToElection}; minus September 4 is {computed.daysPiToElection}; minus September 6 is {computed.daysFilingToElection}; minus September 10 is {computed.daysCa1ToElection}; minus September 13 is {computed.daysNicholsToElection}. September 14 minus September 4 is {computed.daysPiToDenial}; minus September 6 is {computed.daysFilingToDenial}; minus September 10 is {computed.daysCa1ToDenial}; minus September 13 is {computed.daysNicholsToDenial}; minus March 31 is {computed.daysEoToDenial}. September 17 minus September 14 is {computed.daysDenialToSteiner}. Public dissents named in the order PDF: {computed.namedPublicDissents} (Alito, joined by Thomas). Concurrences: {computed.namedConcurrences} (Kavanaugh). Sentences in the unsigned order: {computed.unsignedOrderSentences}.</p>
        </figure>

        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>Who wrote what on September 14</strong><span>From the order PDF and the docket entry.</span></figcaption>
          <DataTable columns={["Writing", "Who", "What it says"]} rows={opinionsTable} highlightColumn={0} />
          <p class="chart-source">Kavanaugh and Alito both write that the Postal Service may have the power to issue the rule, Kavanaugh as "at least a fair prospect" and Alito as "broad authority" over the mail; they part on whether it can run in 2026 and on the pause. The unsigned order does not say which ground made the government unlikely to succeed.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-order" passage="fair prospect that" /><Cite s="scotus-26a305-order" passage="The Postal Service has broad authority to" /><Cite s="scotus-26a305-docket-2026-09-14" passage="The Government is unlikely to succeed on the merits of its challenge to the District Court's preliminary injunction" /></p>
        </figure>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Two questions run under this case: whether the Postal Service can ever impose a rule like this, and whether it could impose this one this close to an election. Kavanaugh's paragraph answers them in that order, possibly yes and no. On the first, he wrote that "there is at least a fair prospect that the final rule falls within the Postal Service's statutory authority"; on the second, that officials lack the time to carry it out before this year's elections. The unsigned order answers neither by name: it says the government is unlikely to succeed and that the factors the Court weighs before granting emergency relief do not favor a pause.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-order" passage="fair prospect that the final rule falls within the Postal Service’s statutory" /><Cite s="scotus-26a305-order" passage="do not have sufficient time to" /><Cite s="scotus-26a305-docket-2026-09-14" passage="do not favor a stay" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The states that sued, led by California, told the Court that the fight is about who runs elections. Under the rule, they wrote, ballots will not be delivered unless each voter is registered in the Postal Service's new portal, a voter-specific barcode is printed on both envelopes, and those barcodes are tied to each voter and uploaded to the portal; with those conditions on delivery, the rule is "an elections regulation". The Constitution, the brief says, "entrusts States with responsibility for regulating federal elections" unless Congress steps in; that provision is the Elections Clause, and it is the ground the First Circuit judged the government's odds on. The government's application called the same requirements modest rules on envelope design and addressee information. Which description is right is the question the appeals will decide.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-california-opp" passage="ballots will not be delivered" /><Cite s="scotus-26a305-california-opp" passage="anything other than an elections" /><Cite s="scotus-26a305-california-opp" passage="entrusts States with responsibility for regulating" /><Cite s="ca1-stay-denial-2026-09-10" passage="We confine our likelihood of success analysis to the constitutional claim based on the" /><Cite s="scotus-26a305-application" passage="imposes only modest envelope-design and addressee-infor" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The portal runs through every step. The rule took effect August 21 with the portal unfinished: on September 1 the Postal Service said it was "now finalizing the portal" and would soon open it to officials who wanted to try it voluntarily.<Cite s="usps-final-rule" passage="Effective August 21, 2026" /><Cite s="usps-portal-statement-2026-09-01" passage="now finalizing the portal" /> Talwani found on September 4 that the portal was not operational, and counted uploading voter data to a portal that did not yet exist among the hurdles facing officials.<Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="The Portal is not currently operational" /><Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="a portal that does not yet exist" /> The First Circuit listed the lack of a working portal among the harm findings it left standing on September 10.<Cite s="ca1-stay-denial-2026-09-10" passage="the lack of a presently operative portal" /> The Court refused to lift the block on September 14.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-docket-2026-09-14" passage="Application (26A305) for stay presented to Justice Jackson and by her referred to the Court is denied" /> On September 17 the Postmaster General told the Associated Press that the agency had stopped work on the system.<Cite s="ap-2026-09-17" passage="has stopped work on developing" /> Whether the portal would have worked is disputed on the record: Whistleblower Aid, representing the employee who raised the alarm, told the Court that the verification standard is zero percent failure, meaning a batch of ballots is treated as non-compliant if one envelope fails the barcode check, and the Postmaster General's September 4 statement said "Testing and validation have been continuous throughout the development lifecycle".<Cite s="scotus-26a305-whistleblower-aid-amicus" passage="zero percent failure" /><Cite s="usps-steiner-statement-2026-09-04" passage="Testing and validation have been continuous throughout the development lifecycle" /> Both courts that blocked the rule for this fall counted the portal's absence, not its quality, among the harms.<Cite s="ca1-stay-denial-2026-09-10" passage="the lack of a presently operative portal" /><Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="a portal that does not yet exist" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">On fraud, the stated purpose of the rule, the First Circuit wrote that there is "no record evidence of past fraud or a likelihood of impending fraud related to the November 3 election".<Cite s="ca1-stay-denial-2026-09-10" passage="no record evidence of past fraud or a likelihood of impending fraud related to the November 3 election" /> The government's reply said the rule would make election fraud harder to commit and easier to catch.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-reply" passage="harder to commit and easier to catch" /> Talwani found the record includes no evidence relating to fraudulent mail voting to support the rushed implementation of the rule; on the record as the courts have read it so far, the harm side of the scale has findings and the fraud side has an argument.<Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="fraudulent mail voting to support the rushed implementation of the Final Rule" /></p>

        <SourcedBlock source="ca1-stay-denial-2026-09-10" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Which ground each court reached. The First Circuit confined its likelihood-of-success analysis to the Elections Clause and did not reach Talwani's separate statutory holdings.<Cite s="ca1-stay-denial-2026-09-10" passage="We confine our likelihood of success analysis to the constitutional claim based on the" /> Nichols granted relief on the ground that no statute grants the Postal Service the power to issue key parts of the rule.<Cite s="nichols-pi-2026-09-13" passage="the Postal Service the power to issue key parts of the Rule" /> Alito wrote that twelve states supported the rule in the lower court and asserted that compliance at this time is practicable.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-order" passage="Twelve States supported the rule below" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="who-feels-it">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Election offices already mailing</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Who feels it</h2>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The people this order lands on are the offices sending ballots for federal general, special and runoff elections, and the voters waiting on them; ballots for military and overseas voters, and primaries, sit outside the rule.<Cite s="usps-final-rule" passage="the rule will not apply to primaries" /> Solicitor General D. John Sauer, the government's lawyer at the Court, told it on September 6 that ballots had already begun to be mailed in North Carolina.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-application" passage="Ballots have already begun to be mailed in North Carolina" /> Current and former election officials, in a brief filed September 9 that supported neither side, wrote that changing the rules now "will almost certainly lead to mistakes, delays, and confusion" for voters and officials.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-election-officials-amicus" passage="mistakes, delays, and confusion" /> Monday's refusal means those offices can keep mailing with the envelopes they have: for elections through November 3, Talwani's order stops the Postal Service from refusing their ballots for not meeting the new rule, and it leaves any state free to comply on its own.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-docket-2026-09-14" passage="Application (26A305) for stay presented to Justice Jackson and by her referred to the Court is denied" /><Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="States from voluntarily complying with the Final Rule" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Talwani found that putting a rule issued less than seventy days before the election into immediate effect "threatens disenfranchisement of millions" of citizens who vote by mail.<Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="threatens disenfranchisement of millions" /> The states that sued told the Court they expect to mail over {computed.leagueStatesBallotsMillion} million ballots between the filing and Election Day, and Nichols wrote that in 2022 the Postal Service delivered at least {computed.uspsDelivered2022Million} million ballot pieces to and from voters.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-lwvma-opp" passage="Plaintiff States anticipate mailing over 50 million ballots" /><Cite s="nichols-pi-2026-09-13" passage="more than fifty million pieces of federal ballot mail" /> The first figure is the states' own forecast and the second is the last midterm's count; neither order counts ballots that would be lost. Nichols found an increased risk that a significant number of otherwise appropriate absentee or mail-in ballots would not be counted, a finding for a preliminary order and no more.<Cite s="nichols-pi-2026-09-13" passage="increased risk that a significant number of otherwise appropriate absentee or mail-in ballots" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Talwani wrote that some states forbid the disclosure of voter information under confidentiality laws protecting victims of domestic abuse or intimate partner violence, and that the rule directly conflicts with those state laws.<Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="forbid the disclosure of voter information" /><Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="directly conflicts with these state laws" /> A brief from domestic-violence organizations told the Court that {computed.acpStates} states and the District of Columbia have some type of address confidentiality program, and that in Colorado the rule was expected to cause the Postal Service to refuse mail ballots to at least {computed.coloradoConfidentialVoters.toLocaleString("en-US")} eligible but confidential voters.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-dv-amicus" passage="45 states and the District of Columbia have some type of ACP" /><Cite s="scotus-26a305-dv-amicus" passage="at least 150,000 eligible but confidential Colorado voters" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">BBC interviewed local officials the week of the ruling. Anne Dover, election director in Cherokee County, Georgia, said the rule changes would have resulted in "thousands of people disenfranchised" there. Josh Zygielbaum, the clerk of Adams County, Colorado, said the day before the ruling that his envelopes were already printed. Both are interviews the BBC published on September 15.<Cite s="bbc-2026-09-15" passage="thousands of people disenfranchised" /><Cite s="bbc-2026-09-15" passage="we already have our envelopes printed" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Had the Court granted the pause, offices with ballots already moving would have faced the envelope and portal requirements mid-mailing. The refusal keeps Talwani's command in force: the Postal Service may not treat those mailings as unaccepted for elections on or before November 3.<Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="elections occurring before or on November 3" /><Cite s="scotus-26a305-docket-2026-09-14" passage="Application (26A305) for stay presented to Justice Jackson and by her referred to the Court is denied" /> And by the Postmaster General's account {computed.daysDenialToSteiner} days later, the agency is no longer building the system it would need to enforce the rule this year.<Cite s="ap-2026-09-17" passage="has stopped work on developing" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="outlets">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Checked against the order</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What the coverage got right, and what it got wrong</h2>
        <p data-layer="fact">Every article on this page is checked below against the order, errors first. In these cards, wrong means the order says otherwise; mislabeled means the outlet named the wrong thing; not in the record means the order neither says it nor denies it; checks out means the order says it.</p>

        <div data-layer="fact">
          <OutletCheck outlet="WIRED" claim={`wrote that Kavanaugh, "in a concurring opinion with Justice Ketanji Brown Jackson," wrote the sentence "The Government is unlikely to succeed on the merits of its challenge to the District Court's preliminary injunction."`} verdict="That sentence is the unsigned order, above Kavanaugh's name. The order names Jackson as the justice the request was presented to, and names no one as joining Kavanaugh's concurrence. Kavanaugh's own paragraph is the fair-prospect line about the Postal Service's power and the 2026 timing." chip="wrong" status="warn"><Cite s="wired-2026-09-14" passage="concurring opinion with Justice Ketanji Brown Jackson" /><Cite s="scotus-26a305-order" passage="JUSTICE KAVANAUGH, concurring" /></OutletCheck>
          <OutletCheck outlet="Washington Examiner" claim={`wrote, in one sentence: "Though the court decided in the Trump administration's favor regarding mail-in ballot restrictions, it won't enforce them for the midterm elections."`} verdict="The Court refused the administration's request. The administration had asked the Court to pause Talwani's block so the rule could run this fall; the order says the request is denied." chip="wrong" status="warn"><Cite s="examiner-2026-09-14" passage="Though the court decided in the Trump administration’s favor regarding mail-in ballot restrictions, it won’t enforce them for the midterm elections." /><Cite s="scotus-26a305-docket-2026-09-14" passage="Application (26A305) for stay presented to Justice Jackson and by her referred to the Court is denied" /></OutletCheck>
          <OutletCheck outlet="BBC News" claim={`wrote that Talwani's September 4 injunction was "blocking Trump's order," and that Alito and Thomas wrote that the challenge to Trump's order was a "Hail Mary pass."`} verdict="The September 4 order blocks parts of the Postal Service's rule. Alito's Hail Mary line is about the states' claim against that rule. The block of the March order itself had been paused on August 24." chip="mislabeled" status="warn"><Cite s="bbc-2026-09-15" passage="blocking Trump's order" /><Cite s="bbc-2026-09-15" passage="the challenge to Trump's order was a &quot;Hail Mary pass&quot;" /><Cite s="scotus-26a124-opinion" passage="We agree and grant the stay." /></OutletCheck>
          <OutletCheck outlet="WIRED and CNBC" claim={`WIRED wrote that the court "was divided 7-2"; CNBC, on September 15, wrote that two public dissents "implies a 7-2 vote to block the Postal Service rule."`} verdict="The order names two dissenters and one concurrence and no other justice's vote. It neither states a 7-2 count nor rules one out; both outlets inferred it from the two names." chip="not in the record" status="note"><Cite s="wired-2026-09-14" passage="divided 7-2" /><Cite s="cnbc-trump-2026-09-15" passage="implies a 7-2 vote" /><Cite s="scotus-26a305-docket-2026-09-14" passage="Justice Alito, joined by Justice Thomas, dissenting" /></OutletCheck>
          <OutletCheckFold count={9} method="the September 14 order PDF, the docket, and the Postal Service's own statements.">
            <OutletCheck folded outlet="SCOTUSblog" claim={`wrote that the justices left in place a Boston judge's ruling in a brief, unsigned order, with a Kavanaugh concurrence on timing and an Alito dissent joined by Thomas.`} verdict="Checks out against the docket entry and the order PDF." chip="checks out" status="ok"><Cite s="scotusblog-2026-09-14" passage="brief, unsigned order" /></OutletCheck>
            <OutletCheck folded outlet="CBS News" claim={`quoted the unlikely-to-succeed sentence, named Alito and Thomas as dissenters, and quoted Kavanaugh's fair-prospect line.`} verdict="Checks out against the order. The 6-3 count CBS gives is for the August 24 ruling on the executive order, a different vote." chip="checks out" status="ok"><Cite s="cbs-2026-09-14" passage="is unlikely to succeed on the merits of its challenge to the District Court's preliminary injunction" /></OutletCheck>
            <OutletCheck folded outlet="Politico" claim={`wrote that the justices left in place a lower-court decision blocking the Postal Service from implementing the new requirements, with a Kavanaugh concurrence and an Alito-Thomas dissent, and noted Nichols's Sunday injunction.`} verdict="Checks out against the order, the docket, and Nichols's September 13 opinion." chip="checks out" status="ok"><Cite s="politico-2026-09-14" passage="left in place a lower-court decision" /></OutletCheck>
            <OutletCheck folded outlet="ABC News" claim={`quoted Kavanaugh's paragraph, with "arbitrary and capricious violation" where the concurrence has the word in before violation.`} verdict="Checks out on the substance; one word dropped, meaning unchanged." chip="checks out" status="ok"><Cite s="abc-2026-09-14" passage="arbitrary and capricious violation of the Administrative Procedure Act" /></OutletCheck>
            <OutletCheck folded outlet="Roll Call" claim={`wrote that the unsigned, one-paragraph order said the government was unlikely to succeed, that Kavanaugh wrote the rule itself may be lawful but could not be carried out before the midterms, and that Alito and Thomas dissented.`} verdict="Checks out against the order and the two signed writings." chip="checks out" status="ok"><Cite s="rollcall-2026-09-14" passage="the rule itself may be lawful" /></OutletCheck>
            <OutletCheck folded outlet="CNBC, Monday night" claim={`quoted the unlikely-to-succeed sentence and named Kavanaugh's concurrence and the Alito-Thomas dissent.`} verdict="Checks out against the unsigned order and the two signed writings." chip="checks out" status="ok"><Cite s="cnbc-2026-09-14" passage="is unlikely to succeed on the merits of its challenge to the" /></OutletCheck>
            <OutletCheck folded outlet="Associated Press, September 15" claim={`wrote that Trump went after the Court after it "rejected his push to restrict mail-in voting" in this year's midterms.`} verdict="Consistent with the refusal. The Tuesday dispatch is Trump's reaction, and it is the president's statement to the public, made after the Court acted." chip="consistent" status="ok"><Cite s="ap-2026-09-14" passage="rejected his push to restrict mail-in voting" /></OutletCheck>
            <OutletCheck folded outlet="NPR" claim={`aired a Tuesday interview in which David Becker said election officials feel a sense of relief and that there was no way to implement these rules in time.`} verdict="Consistent with Kavanaugh's timing sentence. The interview is reaction, and Becker is not a party to the case." chip="consistent" status="ok"><Cite s="npr-2026-09-15" passage="Election officials feel a sense of relief" /></OutletCheck>
            <OutletCheck folded outlet="Associated Press, September 17" claim={`reported that Postmaster General David Steiner said the agency "has stopped work on developing" the computer system, and that a memo to employees said it will "not be enforcing any of these new rules in the 2026 federal election."`} verdict="Consistent with the Postal Service's own election-mail page, which as of September 18 says it is not enforcing the rule for 2026." chip="consistent" status="ok"><Cite s="ap-2026-09-17" passage="has stopped work on developing" /><Cite s="ap-2026-09-17" passage="not be enforcing any of these new rules in the 2026 federal election" /><Cite s="usps-election-mail-page-2026-09-18" passage="Postal Service is not enforcing the federal ballot mail rule for 2026" /></OutletCheck>
