      <section id="claim-check">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">What the Court refused, and what it did not decide</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Claim checked against the record</h2>

        <div data-layer="fact">
          <CheckedBlock label="The coverage's claim: the Supreme Court rejected Trump's mail-ballot restrictions" variant="contested">
            <p>Too broad. The Court refused to pause Talwani's September 4 block on the mandatory parts of the Postal Service rule, for elections on or before November 3. The rule is not struck down. The March executive order was not the thing the Court was asked to pause. Kavanaugh wrote that there is "at least a fair prospect" the rule falls within the Postal Service's legal power.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-order" passage="fair prospect that" /> Alito would have lifted Talwani's block.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-order" passage="JUSTICE ALITO, with whom JUSTICE THOMAS joins" /> The refusal keeps the rule's mandatory parts off this fall's ballots, and states may still comply voluntarily; whether the restrictions are lawful is the question in the government's appeal of Talwani's order, still pending in the First Circuit.<Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="has not and will not order injunctive relief that precludes" /><Cite s="scotus-26a305-application" passage="applicants promptly appealed the district court" /><Cite s="scotus-26a305-docket-2026-09-14" passage="Application (26A305) for stay presented to Justice Jackson and by her referred to the Court is denied" /></p>
          </CheckedBlock>
        </div>

        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>The government's three emergency requests in this fight</strong><span>From the Supreme Court dockets.</span></figcaption>
          <DataTable columns={["Filed", "What it asked", "What the Court did"]} rows={applicationsTable} highlightColumn={0} />
          <p class="chart-source">The Court paused the block of the executive order in August and refused to pause the block of the rule in September.<Cite s="scotus-26a124-opinion" passage="We agree and grant the stay." /><Cite s="scotus-26a305-docket-2026-09-14" passage="Application (26A305) for stay presented to Justice Jackson and by her referred to the Court is denied" /></p>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section id="what-happened-next">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The week after</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened next</h2>

        <p data-layer="narrative">On Tuesday, September 15, Trump wrote on Truth Social that Republicans had just gotten another bad decision from the Court and called it a big loss for Republicans; CNBC and BBC carried the posts.<Cite s="cnbc-trump-2026-09-15" passage="Republicans just got another bad decision" /><Cite s="bbc-2026-09-15" passage="Republicans just got another bad decision" /> The government's request to the Court ran in his name: the cover of the second of its two cases lists "DONALD J. TRUMP, ET AL., APPLICANTS". The posts are statements to the public; the application is the filing.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-application" passage="DONALD J. TRUMP, ET AL., APPLICANTS" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">By September 18 the Postal Service's own election-mail page carried an alert headed "Postal Service is not enforcing the federal ballot mail rule for 2026".<Cite s="usps-election-mail-page-2026-09-18" passage="Postal Service is not enforcing the federal ballot mail rule for 2026" /> That is an operating decision for this year's elections. It does not settle whether the Postal Service has the power to adopt a rule like this for a later election; Kavanaugh wrote there is at least a fair prospect that it does.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-order" passage="fair prospect that the final rule falls within the Postal Service’s statutory" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">On September 17 Postmaster General David Steiner told the Associated Press that the agency "has stopped work on developing" the computer system at the center of the rule, the portal, and that he had planned to unveil it around September 15. A memo sent to all employees the day before, the AP reported, said the agency will not be enforcing any of the new rules in the 2026 federal election and that election mail is business as usual.<Cite s="ap-2026-09-17" passage="has stopped work on developing" /><Cite s="ap-2026-09-17" passage="had planned to unveil it around Sept. 15" /><Cite s="ap-2026-09-17" passage="not be enforcing any of these new rules in the 2026 federal election" /> On September 1 the same agency had said it was finalizing the portal and would soon open it to officials who wanted to try it.<Cite s="usps-portal-statement-2026-09-01" passage="now finalizing the portal" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The Associated Press also reported "there are still concerns about postmark delays that could have an impact on this year’s elections," and that late-postmark rejections had risen in Washington and Oregon.<Cite s="ap-2026-09-17" passage="there are still concerns about postmark delays that could have an impact on this year’s elections" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Watching: the First Circuit's decision on the appeal of Talwani's order; whether the government asks a court to lift Nichols's order, which blocks the whole rule with no election-date limit, so the rule as issued cannot start after November 3 unless a court lifts it; and the inspector general's review of the whistleblower's allegations, which its office said on September 8 it planned to conduct.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-supplemental" passage="from implementing and enforcing the Final Rule" /><Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="elections occurring before or on November 3" /><Cite s="democracydocket-oig-2026-09-08" passage="plan to conduct an independent review of these allegations" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">On September 4 the inspector general published a separate audit of 2026 primary-election mail, with on-time processing scores ranging from {computed.oigOnTimeLow} to {computed.oigOnTimeHigh} percent.<Cite s="uspsoig-primary-audit-2026" passage="97.75 to 98.99 percent" /> The audit made {computed.oigRecommendations} recommendations to address the issues identified in the report.<Cite s="uspsoig-primary-audit-2026" passage="We made 11 recommendations" /> It found opportunities to increase understanding and enforcement of postmarking ballots, and that communication with customers affected by operational changes could be strengthened.<Cite s="uspsoig-primary-audit-2026" passage="increase understanding and enforcement of postmarking ballots" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="unknowns">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Open questions</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What we do not know yet</h2>
        <ul data-layer="fact">
          <li data-absence>How the six justices the order does not name voted. The September 14 order PDF and the docket name Kavanaugh in concurrence and Alito joined by Thomas in dissent; they do not name Roberts, Gorsuch, Barrett, Sotomayor, Kagan or Jackson on the refusal. Searched: the order PDF; the docket entry for September 14.</li>
          <li data-absence>Whether the government has asked the D.C. Circuit or the Supreme Court to lift Nichols's September 13 order. Searched: the Supreme Court docket for this case as read on September 18; it lists the refusal and no request about Nichols's order.</li>
          <li data-absence>Whether the Postal Service inspector general has published the independent review of the whistleblower allegations its office said on September 8 it planned to conduct. Searched: the inspector general's report listings saved for the earlier story; the news captures for this story through September 18.</li>
          <li>Which five states Sauer meant by the week of September 13 in the September 6 application. The application does not name them.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-application" passage="Ballots have already begun to be mailed in North Carolina" /></li>
        </ul>
      </section>

